2026-08-25  Arka Mondal  <arkamondalofficial@gmail.com>

	Version 0.2.0.

	* src/holes.c: New file.
	(hole_next_data): New function.  Reports the written extents of a
	descriptor.
	(hole_tail): New function.  Reports a file whose last byte lies in
	an unwritten region.
	* src/template.c: New file.  Holds what a new file starts out
	with, taken out of fileops.c.
	(tmpl_open): New function.  Settles the source once for the run.
	A regular source with a usable size is copied extent by extent,
	anything else is read straight through, or read into memory first
	where several files need it.  The operand count reaches only that
	second case.  Standard input takes the extent path only where
	nothing has been read from it yet, since what is left of a partly
	read one has no layout to reproduce.
	(tmpl_apply, tmpl_tail_hole, tmpl_close): New functions.
	(copy_extents): New function.  Copies the written extents of a
	source to the same offsets, stopping where st_size stands in for
	the content.
	(copy_stream): New function.  Carries the former read and write
	loop.
	(write_at, io_bufsize, io_bufalloc): New functions.  io_bufsize
	takes the larger of the two descriptors a copy moves between.
	(IO_BUFSIZE_MIN, IO_BUFSIZE_MAX): New macros, replacing IO_BUFSIZE.
	* src/fastio.c (fast_alloc): Take an offset as well as a length.
	(fast_copy): Take both offsets and a length, and advance the
	offsets by the amount transferred.  Move the source checks to the
	caller.
	* src/fileops.c (write_buf, read_all): Move to template.c and make
	static.
	(fill_file): Move to template.c as fill_range, taking an offset and
	addressing the file rather than relying on its offset.
	(sync_dir): Remove.  dirsync_stage, dirsync_flush, dirsync_dir and
	dirsync_release replace it, one flush covering a run of renames
	into one directory.
	(dir_of): New function.
	* src/main.c (apply_size): New function.  Truncates or extends to
	the requested size.
	(dirsync_error): New function.  Both reports of a failed directory
	flush go through it.  A failure that comes before any directory is
	open names the operand instead.
	(create_one): Copy the template before applying the size.  Stage
	the directory flush rather than performing one per operand.
	(main): Open the template before the operand loop and close it
	after.  A template that cannot be read ends the run before any file
	is created.  Flush the staged directories after the loop, then
	release the last of them.
	(parse_options): Drop the rejection of --template with --size.
	(struct file_spec): Drop stdin_buf, stdin_len and stdin_buffered.
	Add tmpl in place of template_given.
	* src/options.c (notes): Break the backup control values at the
	entry boundary, in place of one entry spanning two literals.
	* src/newfile.h: Update the declarations.
	(ATTR_UNUSED): New macro.  Marks the parameters the fallback stubs
	cannot use.
	* Makefile (VERSION): 0.2.0.
	(OBJS): Add holes.o and template.o.
	(DISTFILES): Add bench.
	* doc/newfile.1: Document the layout a template gives the copy, how
	--size and --sparse act on it, that the template is opened once for
	the run, and which sources a layout is reproduced from.
	* tests/run_tests.sh: Assert the copy's layout under both builds, a
	template read from a pipe, a template redirected from a file
	keeping its layout whatever the operand count, a source with no
	written extent at all, a piped source outgrowing the buffer several
	files share, standard input resuming where it was left, and more
	operands than one directory flush covers.  Drop the assertion that
	--template and --size are rejected together.
	* bench/run_bench.sh: New file.
	* README.md: Describe the benchmark script and what the test suite
	covers.

